Subject: DRUG this week: Wickson 1017 (Fri 10-12)
Message-ID: <G8Y2j3cQ38A.IPolL4lAlEQJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/G8Y2j3cQ38A/m/IPolL4lAlEQJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This week's DRUG meeting will be in Wickson 1017.  It will be there next week (10/19) as well, and then starting 10/26 we will be in PES 2005 for the remainder of the quarter.

For the first part of our work session, Jacob Teter (TTP) will present a "Show and Tell" from about 10-10:30.  From Jacob:

> This stretch of script takes daily resolution climate (max/min temp., precipitation, and derived reference evapotranspiration) data <http://rda.ucar.edu/datasets/ds512.0/metadata/detailed.html> - creates a raster field using thin-plate splines (and potentially also calculates standard error), then combines it with rasterized soil texture data (from SSURGO <http://soildatamart.nrcs.usda.gov/>), and satellite-derived location data on cropping patterns <http://nassgeodata.gmu.edu/CropScape/> for economically important crops. After joining the fields from these three data sources (climate, soil, cropping patterns) in the same coordinate reference system and resolution, we write them out into a simple data.frame that can then be used as inputs for a process-based crop-water-soil-atmosphere model (AquaCrop).
>
> The spatial components of this workflow will be illustrated using data from Kansas.
>
> I'll also ask about better data sources and better practices for making the above sequence of steps happen efficiently.

See you Friday,

Noam

From u8c8f0ca54c4a2459 at groups.google.com  Tue Oct  9 16:05:14 2012
From: u8c8f0ca54c4a2459 at groups.google.com (Noam Ross)
Date: Tue, 09 Oct 2012 16:05:14 +0000
Subject: Future topics for work sessions
Message-ID: <b2eyQj6PMbM.tY8StLhpbMcJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/b2eyQj6PMbM/m/tY8StLhpbMcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

We're looking for people to present in future weeks of our Friday work sessions.  These are informal, ~20-30 minute talks.  Let me know if you are interested.

Talks can be any of these formats:

 - "How to" - A quick introduction to a topic and pointers to more resources
 - "Show and Tell" - A cool thing you did in R
 - "Here's my problem" - take the group through what you're working on and soliciting feedback

For "How to", I would encourage people to pick topics that they might not know a lot about, but would like to spend a bit of time collecting info and presenting resources (packages, tutorials, etc.)

Here are some topics brought up at our first meeting that people might want to discuss:

 - Best code organization practices (style guide)
 - Efficiency in coding - how to speed up your code
 - Options for coding interfaces (notepad++, RStudio...)
 - Version control (git)
 - Mixed models/GAMs
 - Bayesian stats in R
 - Spatial analytics in R
 - Genetics/bioinformatics in R
 - Population modeling
 - Producing reports with knitr/sweave
 - Modeling admixture hybridization packages...
 - MCMC and the STAN package
 - Monte Carlo simulations in R
 - Interfacing with other languages (why and when?)
 - Working with LOTS of data more efficiently
 - Cluster computing and parallel processing

I'm looking forward to a lot of interesting talks!

Noam

